"""Append part B of bilingual C01: secI_2, secI_3, secI_4."""
out = open('Healing_Bilingual/ops/xhtml/C01_chapter.xhtml', 'a', encoding='utf-8')
out.write('''\
<div class="h3_div">
<h3 class="h3" id="secI_2">Fragmentation and Internal Struggles</h3>
<h3 class="h3 trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">碎裂化与内在挣扎</h3>
<p class="noindent">Ten years ago, in the context of consulting with traumatized clients who came to me as an &#8220;expert,&#8221; seeking to understand why they were not making progress in treatment, I began to observe a very characteristic pattern: these clients had something unique in common. Each was superficially an integrated whole person but also manifested clear-cut signs of being internally fragmented. They experienced intense conflicts between trauma-related perceptions and impulses (for example, &#8220;the worst is going to happen,&#8221; &#8220;I will be abandoned if I don&#8217;t get out first&#8221;) versus here-and-now assessments of danger: &#8220;I know I&#8217;m safe here. I wouldn&#8217;t let my children live in this house if it were not safe.&#8221; They suffered from paradoxical symptoms: the desire to be kind and compassionate toward others or to live a spiritual life, on the one hand, and intense rage or even impulses to violence, on the other. Once their conflicts were described, the patterns became more easily observable and meaningful. Each side of the conflict spoke to a different way of surviving the unsurvivable, of reconciling the opposites that are so often part and parcel of traumatic experience. With an explanatory model that described each reaction as logical and necessary in the face of threat or abandonment and that reframed them as the survival responses of different parts of the self, to which the individual could relate, each client started to make faster, more sustainable progress. The theoretical model that best explained the phenomena they described was the Structural Dissociation model of Onno van der Hart, Ellert Nijenhuis, and Kathy Steele (2004). Rooted in a neuroscience perspective and well-accepted throughout Europe as a trauma model, it was a good fit for me as a firm believer and spokesperson for a neurobiologically informed approach to trauma and trauma treatment. The theory describes (Van der Hart, Nijenhuis &amp; Steele, 2006) how the brain&#8217;s innate physical structure and two separate, specialized hemispheres facilitate left brain-right brain disconnection under conditions of threat. Capitalizing on the tendency of the left brain to remain positive, task-oriented, and logical under stress, these writers hypothesized that the disconnected left brain side of the personality stays focused on the tasks of daily living, while the other hemisphere fosters an implicit right brain self that remains in survival mode, braced for danger, ready to run, frozen in fear, praying for rescue, or too ashamed to do anything but submit. In each individual client, I could see that some parts were easier to identify with or &#8220;own&#8221; and some parts were easier to ignore or dismiss as &#8220;not me.&#8221; Internally, the parts were also in conflict: was it safer to freeze or fight? To cry for help? Or to be seen and not heard? What I also noticed was that the internal relationships between these fragmented aspects of self reflected the traumatic environments for which they had once been solutions. The left-brain-dominant <span epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_5"/>present-oriented self avoids the right-brain-dominant survival-oriented parts or judges them as bad qualities to be modified, while the right brain implicit selves of the parts are equally alienated from what they perceive as a &#8220;weak&#8221; or absent other half. The functioning self carries on, trying desperately to be &#8220;normal&#8221;&#8212;at the cost of feeling alienated from or invaded by the intrusive communications of the parts.</p>
<p class="noindent trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">十年前，在与创伤来访者的咨询过程中——他们将我视为"专家"寻求帮助，试图理解为何在治疗中毫无进展——我开始观察到一种极具特征性的模式：这些来访者有一种独特的共同之处。每个人表面上都是一个完整的整体，但同时也表现出内在碎裂化的明确迹象。他们经历着创伤相关感知和冲动（例如，"最坏的情况即将发生"，"如果我不先逃走，我会被遗弃"）与对当下危险的现实评估之间的激烈冲突："我知道我在这里是安全的。如果这所房子不安全，我不会让我的孩子住在这里。"他们承受着悖论性的症状：一方面渴望对他人仁慈、充满慈悲，或过一种精神性的生活，另一方面却有着强烈的愤怒，甚至暴力冲动。一旦他们的冲突被描述出来，这些模式就变得更容易观察和理解。冲突的每一方，都讲述着一种在无法生存中生存下来的不同方式，一种调和那些往往是创伤体验一部分的对立面的方式。有了一个能够将每种反应描述为在威胁或遗弃面前合乎逻辑且必要的解释性模型，并将其重构为个体可以认同的自我不同部分的生存反应，每位来访者都开始取得更快、更可持续的进展。最能解释他们所描述现象的理论模型，是Onno van der Hart、Ellert Nijenhuis和Kathy Steele（2004）的结构性解离模型。这一模型植根于神经科学视角，在欧洲作为创伤模型被广泛接受，对于我这位神经生物学知情创伤治疗方法的坚定信奉者和倡导者而言，它非常契合。该理论描述了（Van der Hart, Nijenhuis和Steele，2006）大脑固有的物理结构和两个独立的专业化半球如何在威胁条件下促进左脑-右脑的断联。利用左脑在压力下保持积极、任务导向和逻辑性的倾向，这些作者假设，断联的人格左脑一侧始终专注于日常生活的任务，而另一半球则滋养着一个内隐的右脑自我，它停留在生存模式中，为危险做好准备，随时准备逃跑，冻结在恐惧中，祈求救援，或因太过羞耻而只能顺从。在每位来访者身上，我都能看到某些部分更容易被认同或"认领"，而另一些部分则更容易被忽视或否定为"不是我"。在内部，各部分之间也存在冲突：冻结还是战斗，哪个更安全？哭泣求助吗？还是被看见却不被倾听？我还注意到，这些碎裂化的自我方面之间的内在关系，反映了它们曾经作为解决方案的那个创伤性环境。左脑主导的面向当下的自我，回避或将右脑主导的生存导向部分评判为需要改变的不良品质，而各部分的右脑内隐自我，同样疏离于它们所感知到的"软弱"或缺席的另一半。功能性自我继续前行，拼命试图"正常"——代价是感到被疏离，或被各部分的侵入性沟通所入侵。</p>
</div>
<div class="h3_div">
<h3 class="h3" id="secI_3">The Price of Self-Alienation: A &#8220;False Self&#8221;</h3>
<h3 class="h3 trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">自我疏离的代价："虚假自我"</h3>
<p class="noindent">Survivors of abuse, neglect, and other traumatic experiences often report functioning better as a result of their compartmentalization but then suffer from feelings of fraudulence or &#8220;pretending.&#8221; Not realizing that each side of the personality is equally &#8220;real&#8221; and necessary from an evolutionary standpoint, clients easily misinterpret the intense, palpable feeling memories of the &#8220;not me&#8221; child as more &#8220;real&#8221; than the experience of the &#8220;going on with normal life self,&#8221; doggedly &#8220;putting one foot in front of the other,&#8221; or &#8220;keeping on keeping on&#8221; even in the face of overwhelming pain. Without an explanatory paradigm that makes sense of these contradictions, there is no way for individuals to know that their intense feelings and distorted perceptions are evidence of fragmentation, not proof of internal defectiveness or fraudulence masked by the ability to function.</p>
<p class="noindent trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">遭受虐待、忽视和其他创伤性体验的幸存者，往往报告说由于自己的分隔化而功能运作得更好，但随后却遭受着欺诈感或"假装"感的折磨。由于没有意识到人格的每一面从进化角度来说都同样是"真实"且必要的，来访者容易将"非我"的儿童那些强烈、可感知的感受记忆，误读为比"正常生活中的自我"的体验更加"真实"——那个倔强地"一步一步向前走"、或即使面对排山倒海的痛苦也"坚持下去"的自我。如果没有一个能够解释这些矛盾的解释性范式，个体就无从知晓，他们强烈的感受和扭曲的感知是碎裂化的证据，而非由功能运作能力所掩盖的内在缺陷性或欺诈性的证明。</p>
<p class="indent">Over time, self-alienation can only be maintained by most individuals at the cost of increasingly greater self-loathing, disconnection from emotion, addictive or self-destructive behavior, and internal struggles between vulnerability and control, love and hate, closeness and distance, shame and pride. While longing to be loved, safe, and welcome, many traumatized clients find themselves alternating between anxious clinging and pushing others away, hating themselves or having little patience with the flaws of others, yearning to be seen and yearning to be invisible. Years later, they present in therapy with symptoms of anxiety, chronic depression, low self-esteem, stuckness in life, or diagnoses such as PTSD, bipolar disorder, borderline personality disorder, even dissociative disorders. Unaware that their symptoms are being driven not just by the traumatic events but by an internal attachment disorder mirroring the traumatic attachment of early childhood, therapist and client have no framework for understanding the chaos and/or stuckness that may soon elude their best efforts at treatment.</p>
<p class="indent trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">随着时间推移，对大多数个体而言，维持自我疏离只能以越来越强烈的自我厌恶、与情绪的断联、成瘾或自毁行为，以及脆弱与控制、爱与恨、亲近与距离、羞耻与自尊之间的内在挣扎为代价。尽管渴望被爱、安全和被接纳，许多受创伤的来访者却发现自己在焦虑地依附他人与推开他人之间交替摇摆，对自己充满憎恨或对他人的缺陷缺乏耐心，既渴望被看见又渴望隐于无形。多年以后，他们带着焦虑症状、慢性抑郁、低自尊、生活停滞，或PTSD、双相障碍、边缘型人格障碍甚至解离障碍等诊断出现在治疗中。治疗师和来访者都没有意识到，症状不仅仅被创伤事件驱动，还被一种内在依恋障碍所驱动——这种障碍映射着早期童年的创伤性依恋——因而对于可能很快超出他们最佳治疗努力的混乱和/或停滞，他们没有任何理解框架。</p>
</div>
<div class="h3_div">
<h3 class="h3" id="secI_4">Traumatic Attachment as a Complication in Trauma Therapy</h3>
<h3 class="h3 trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">创伤性依恋作为创伤治疗中的复杂因素</h3>
<p class="noindent">In the trauma treatment world in which I have practiced professionally over the past 25 years, generations of &#8220;best treatment&#8221; models have been repeatedly challenged by client vulnerability to being triggered by apparently innocuous stimuli, swept into the &#8220;trauma vortex,&#8221; and overwhelmed with painful emotions and physiological responses. For some clients, the present feels little <span epub:type="pagebreak" id="page_6"/>better than the past. Since my postdoctoral fellowship in Judith Herman&#8217;s clinic in 1991 and arrival at Bessel van der Kolk&#8217;s Trauma Center in 1995 as a supervisor and instructor, my colleagues and I, led by Bessel van der Kolk, have been on a quest to find new methods or interventions that might help free our clients from the insidious impact of the traumatic past&#8212;but, even as each is an improvement on the last, we always come up somewhat short. Each new understanding or sophisticated treatment method helps some clients we haven&#8217;t before been able to reach, but it doesn&#8217;t bring resolution for all&#8212;or each brings relief in some symptoms while not alleviating others. And for some traumatized clients, the course of treatment even over the long term seems to consist of two effortful steps forward followed by a slide backward&#8212;pushing the proverbial boulder up a steep hill this week only to find by the next session that it is right back at the bottom again. Even more challenging, some clients find that their trauma-related wishes and fears of relationship are so equally intense that therapy and the therapist evoke painful yearning, mistrust, hypervigilance, and anger, or fear and shame, rather than feelings of safety and comfort. It was my hope in writing this book that the treatment approach described here would create a way for these clients and their therapists to navigate these challenges and resolve them.</p>
<p class="noindent trans-zh" lang="zh-CN">在我执业二十五年的创伤治疗世界里，一代又一代的"最佳治疗"模式被反复挑战——来访者极易被表面上看似无害的刺激触发，被卷入"创伤漩涡"，被痛苦的情绪和生理反应所淹没。对某些来访者而言，当下的感受与过去相比好不了多少。自1991年在Judith Herman诊所完成博士后研究，以及1995年以督导和讲师身份加入Bessel van der Kolk的创伤中心以来，我和同事们在Bessel van der Kolk的带领下，一直在寻求能够帮助来访者从创伤过去的潜移默化影响中解脱出来的新方法或干预手段——但即便每一种都是对上一种的改进，我们总还是有所欠缺。每一种新的理解或精密的治疗方法，都能帮助我们以前无法触及的一些来访者，但并不能为所有人带来解决——或者每一种都能缓解某些症状，却无法减轻其他症状。对某些受创伤的来访者而言，即便是长期的治疗过程，似乎也只是由两步艰难的前进接着一次滑落所构成——本周费尽力气将那块众所周知的巨石推上陡峭的山坡，下一次治疗却发现它又滚回了底部。更具挑战性的是，一些来访者发现他们与关系有关的愿望和恐惧同样强烈，以至于治疗和治疗师唤起的是痛苦的渴望、不信任、高度警觉和愤怒，或恐惧和羞耻，而非安全与舒适的感觉。撰写本书时，我的希望是，书中描述的治疗方法能为这些来访者及其治疗师提供一条应对并化解这些挑战的路径。</p>
</div>
''')
out.close()
